{# The Anthology Atlas, as a reusable body (#1255).

   Extracted from anthology-atlas-page.njk so the canonical /anthology-atlas.html
   and the 17 per-theme pages render the SAME map from one source rather than
   17 hand-wired copies (rule §16.4).

   `atlasTheme` is set by the per-theme pages and absent on the canonical page.
   When present it pre-filters the map to that theme and adjusts the masthead,
   so a crawler and a reader both get the filtered view without a flash of the
   unfiltered map. #}
{% from "icons.njk" import icon %}
{#- Every string on this page comes from the catalog (#1495). `a` is the
    Atlas block for the page's locale, and the JSON blob further down hands
    the same block to anthology-atlas.js for the strings it injects. -#}
{%- set a = i18n[lang or "en"].atlas -%}
{#- The Atlas exists in three languages (#1495). The canonical page resolves
    through localizedHref, which checks the source file exists. The theme
    pages are generated by pagination, so no source file exists for the
    checker to find and their locale suffix is built here instead. -#}
{%- set atlasHref = "/anthology-atlas.html" | localizedHref(lang) -%}
{%- set themeSuffix = "" if (lang or "en") == "en" else ("." + lang) -%}
{%- set L = lang or "en" -%}
{#- The theme vocabulary is translated (#1492), and the year range needs the
    locale's own word for "to" rather than an English one built in the data. -#}
{%- set themeLabel = (atlasTheme.label[L] or atlasTheme.label.en) if atlasTheme else "" -%}
{%- set themeRange = "" -%}
{%- if atlasTheme -%}
  {%- if atlasTheme.firstYear == atlasTheme.lastYear -%}
    {%- set themeRange = "" ~ atlasTheme.firstYear -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- set themeRange = "" ~ atlasTheme.firstYear ~ " " ~ a.yearRangeJoiner ~ " " ~ atlasTheme.lastYear -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{# The share card is the map itself (#1156), and it is generated now rather
   than captured by hand (#1545): scripts/atlas_map_card.py redraws the corpus
   with the same seeded layout, forces and colours this page uses, and
   scripts/make-share-cards.py rasterises it through the same qlmanage
   pipeline as every other card. Refresh all three with

     python3 scripts/make-share-cards.py anthology-atlas

   when the corpus changes visibly, e.g. after a new edition lands. One card
   per locale, because the hub labels on the map are the reader's language
   since #1495 and a screenshot cannot be translated. #}
{# Anthology Atlas (#1124, #1129). Promoted out of soft launch (#1134): now
   indexed (no noindex), in the sitemap (no eleventyExcludeFromCollections),
   in site search (no data-pagefind-ignore), and signposted from the Anthology
   in every locale. Still EN-only. The graph is the real
   /data/anthology-atlas.json, projected from paperIndex.js at build time.
   Sibling of the NetSec Atlas (people); this one maps papers. #}
{# First-visit pre-paint reveal: stamp html.atlas-welcome before the welcome
   strip is parsed, so it occupies its space from the first frame instead of
   popping in when the deferred atlas script runs (the anti-CLS trick from the
   NetSec directory). anthology-atlas.js reconciles the hidden attribute and
   drops the class once it takes over, and on dismissal. #}
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      if (localStorage.getItem('eiss-atlas-tour-seen') !== 'true') {
        document.documentElement.classList.add('atlas-welcome');
      }
    } catch (e) {}
  })();
</script>
{# data-atlas-theme is the pre-filter (#1255). anthology-atlas.js reads it in
   applyUrlState(), before the chips render, so the map arrives already filtered
   rather than flashing the full corpus first. The URL still wins if someone
   deep-links a different filter on top. #}
<div class="atlas-shell"{% if atlasTheme %} data-atlas-theme="{{ atlasTheme.name }}"{% endif %}>
  {% if atlasTheme %}<a class="atlas-back" href="{{ atlasHref }}">&larr; {{ a.backWhole }}</a>
  {% else %}<a class="atlas-back" href="{{ '/anthology.html' | localizedHref(lang) }}">&larr; {{ a.backAnthology }}</a>{% endif %}
  <div class="atlas-masthead">
    {# The shared Anthology/Atlas mark. Decorative here: the h1 beside it already
       names the page, so the wrapper is aria-hidden. #}
    <span class="atlas-mark" aria-hidden="true">{% inlineSvg "assets/images/brand/anthology-mark.svg" %}</span>
    <h1>{% if atlasTheme %}{{ themeLabel }}{% else %}{{ a.title }}{% endif %}</h1>
    {#- The pill is the interface half of the declared scope (#1499). It goes
        when releaseScope.interfaceReady says the chrome, the controls and the
        theme labels are finished work, which is a judgement rather than a
        measurement and so is the maintainer's to make. -#}
    {%- if not releaseScope.interfaceReady %}<span class="atlas-proto-pill">{{ a.beta }}</span>{% endif %}
    <button class="atlas-help" type="button" id="atlas-help" title="{{ a.helpTitle }}" aria-label="{{ a.helpTitle }}">?</button>
  </div>
  {# The lede says what the map is, and stops there. The mechanics it used to
     carry (solid versus hollow dots, the filter chips, the two lenses in
     detail) are all on the page already: the legend sits under the map, the
     welcome bar greets a first visit, and the tour walks through the rest.
     The closing "read it by paper or by author, and hover any dot" went the
     same way in #1459: the lens chips are directly above the map, the tour
     and the welcome bar both say it, and "hover" stopped being the gesture
     on a phone when a tap started pinning the card (#1431). Dropping it is
     what puts the map on screen at 375px on a first visit. #}
  {% if atlasTheme %}
  {# Theme pages get their own lede rather than the generic one. #1255 flags
     these as near-duplicates of each other in body content, and a distinct
     opening paragraph carrying the theme's own figures is what keeps them
     genuinely distinct rather than 17 copies with a different H1. #}
  {%- set anthologyLink = '<a href="' + ('/anthology.html' | localizedHref(lang)) + '">' + a.ledeLinkText + '</a>' -%}
  <p class="atlas-lede">{{ a.ledeTheme | replace("{count}", atlasTheme.count) | replace("{lower}", themeLabel | lower) | replace("{range}", themeRange) | replace("{authors}", atlasTheme.authorCount) | replace("{link}", anthologyLink) | safe }}</p>
  {% else %}
  {%- set anthologyLink = '<a href="' + ('/anthology.html' | localizedHref(lang)) + '">' + a.ledeLinkText + '</a>' -%}
  <p class="atlas-lede">{{ a.ledeWhole | replace("{link}", anthologyLink) | safe }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  {# First-visit welcome strip (#1134). Rendered hidden; the inline head
     script above reveals it pre-paint on a first visit, and anthology-
     atlas.js reconciles the hidden attribute + wires the buttons. #}
  <aside class="atlas-welcome" id="atlas-welcome" aria-label="{{ a.welcomeAria }}" hidden>
    {# One bar rather than the four-tip block it used to be (#1444). The tips
       taught the lenses, the chips, the dot markers and the click-through,
       and the tour teaches all four to the same reader a moment later, so
       the strip was holding 251px of the first screen to say twice what the
       tour says once. The map is what a first visit should open on. #}
    <p class="atlas-welcome-lede">{{ a.welcomeLede | replace("{papers}", anthologyAtlas.papers | length) | replace("{editions}", anthologyAtlas.years | length) | replace("{themes}", anthologyAtlas.themes | length) }}</p>
    <div class="atlas-welcome-actions">
      <button class="tour-btn tour-btn-primary" type="button" id="atlas-welcome-tour">{{ a.welcomeTour }}</button>
      <button class="atlas-welcome-dismiss" type="button" id="atlas-welcome-dismiss" aria-controls="atlas-welcome">{{ a.welcomeDismiss }}</button>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-lens" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.lensAria }}">
    <span class="lbl">{{ a.lensLabel }}</span>
    <!-- lens chips (Papers / Authors) injected by anthology-atlas.js -->
  </div>


  <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-authoropts" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.authorOptsAria }}" hidden>
    <!-- "Collaborators only" chip injected by anthology-atlas.js (Authors lens only) -->
  </div>

  <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-paperopts" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.paperOptsAria }}">
    <!-- "Abstract on file" chip injected by anthology-atlas.js (Papers lens only, #1152) -->
  </div>

  <div class="atlas-controls" role="search" aria-label="{{ a.findAria }}">
    <label class="lbl" for="atlas-find">{{ a.findLabel }}</label>
    <input class="atlas-find" id="atlas-find" type="search" list="atlas-find-list" placeholder="{{ a.findPlaceholder }}" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
    <datalist id="atlas-find-list"><!-- options injected by anthology-atlas.js (#1154) --></datalist>
    {# Typing a name that is not in the corpus, or one the current filters
       hide, used to do nothing at all. #}
    <span class="atlas-find__msg" id="atlas-find-msg" role="status"></span>
  </div>

  {# Copy-link (#1445). The lens, the chips, the toggles and the current
     spotlight have been mirrored into the query string since #1151, so a
     filtered view has always been shareable and nothing on the page said so.
     The row appears only once the view differs from the default map, so the
     canonical Atlas does not carry a button that copies its own plain URL. #}
  <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-shareopts" hidden>
    <button class="atlas-share" type="button" id="atlas-share">{{ a.shareCopy }}</button>
    {# Clear used to live inside the filters <summary>. A <summary> is itself
       the disclosure control, so a button inside it is a control inside a
       control: axe reports nested-interactive, and a press had to fight the
       disclosure for the click (#1625). It belongs here anyway, beside the
       other action on the current view. This row is shown whenever the view
       differs from the default map, which is a superset of the cases where
       Clear has anything to clear, so it always has somewhere to appear. #}
    <button class="atlas-filters__clear" type="button" id="atlas-filters-clear" hidden>{{ a.filtersClear }}</button>
  </div>

  {# Filter rows (#1191). A native <details>, open in the markup so the
     no-JS case simply shows everything. anthology-atlas.js then collapses it
     at every width (#1430, extending #1191's phones-only behaviour): 12
     edition chips plus 18 theme chips put the top of the map at y=1074 on a
     1280x900 desktop too. The summary carries the active-filter count and is
     a visible control at every width since #1508. #}
  <details class="atlas-filters" id="atlas-filters" open>
    {# The label is its own element so the active-filter count can be rewritten
       independently. Clear itself sits in the view-actions row above, and
       still only appears once something is actually filtering (#1442). #}
    {# The summary is a control, so it looks like one (#1508). It read as a
       section heading with a rule under it and no marker, because a
       `display: flex` summary loses the browser's own disclosure triangle,
       and readers did not know the filters were there at all. Chevron,
       hover and focus states, and a label that says what opens. #}
    <summary class="atlas-filters__summary" id="atlas-filters-summary">
      <span class="atlas-filters__chevron" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("chevron-down") }}</span>
      <span id="atlas-filters-label">{{ a.filtersLabel }}</span>
      <span class="atlas-filters__hint" id="atlas-filters-hint">{{ a.filtersHint }}</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-years" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.editionsAria }}">
      <span class="lbl">{{ a.editionsLabel }}</span>
      {# Every chip starts on, so isolating one edition meant switching off the
         other eleven by hand (#1442). These two do it in one press. #}
      <span class="atlas-bulk" id="atlas-years-bulk"><!-- All / None injected by anthology-atlas.js --></span>
      <!-- year chips injected by anthology-atlas.js -->
    </div>
    <div class="atlas-controls" id="atlas-themes" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.themesAria }}">
      <span class="lbl">{{ a.themesLabel }}</span>
      <span class="atlas-bulk" id="atlas-themes-bulk"><!-- All / None injected by anthology-atlas.js --></span>
      <!-- theme hub chips injected by anthology-atlas.js -->
    </div>
  </details>

  {# Announcements for anyone who cannot see the map move: the card pinned by
     a tap, and the node a search lands on (#1446). Visually hidden, so it
     costs the sighted reader nothing. #}
  <p class="sr-only" role="status" id="atlas-live"></p>

  {# The per-theme facts the build already computes for the 17 theme pages:
     name, slug, paper count, year range, author count (#1255,
     src/_data/atlasThemePages.js). The hub panel reads them straight out of
     here, so the slug rule lives in one place and the panel cannot disagree
     with the page it links to. About 2KB. #}
  <script type="application/json" id="atlas-theme-index">{{ atlasThemePages | dump | safe }}</script>

  {# The same catalog the markup above uses, handed to anthology-atlas.js for
     the strings it injects: the chips, the cards, the hub panel, the findings,
     the list headers and the tour (#1495). One source, so the map cannot
     speak two languages at once. #}
  <script type="application/json" id="atlas-strings">{{ a | dump | safe }}</script>

  <div class="atlas-stage">
    {# Zoom cluster (#1433). The map holds around a thousand dots in 640px of
       height, and the clusters around the busier hubs were unreadable without
       a way to look closer. Reset also puts dragged theme hubs back where the
       layout seeded them (#1434), and only appears once there is something to
       reset. #}
    <div class="atlas-zoom" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.zoomAria }}">
      <button class="atlas-zoom__b" type="button" id="atlas-zoom-in" aria-label="{{ a.zoomIn }}">+</button>
      <button class="atlas-zoom__b" type="button" id="atlas-zoom-out" aria-label="{{ a.zoomOut }}" disabled>&minus;</button>
      <button class="atlas-zoom__b atlas-zoom__reset" type="button" id="atlas-zoom-reset" hidden>{{ a.zoomReset }}</button>
    </div>
    {# tabindex + a described hint (#1646). The map was one tab stop short of
       being operable: the chips, the Find box and the list under it all took
       keyboard input and the canvas itself took none, so its nodes were
       pointer-only. The arrow keys now walk the same order the list uses. #}
    <canvas id="atlas-canvas" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="atlas-kbd-hint" aria-label="Force-directed map of the European Security Studies Anthology: every paper drawn toward the research themes it touches and coloured by them, with an Authors lens mapping co-authorship between the same people. The same papers and authors are available as filterable lists on the Anthology page."></canvas>
    <p class="sr-only" id="atlas-kbd-hint">{{ a.kbdHint }}</p>
    <div class="atlas-card" id="atlas-card" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>
  {# The corpus over time (#1192). Parked when it was filed, because themes
     were derived from panel titles and the whole corpus held about 30
     distinct theme signatures, so stepping through editions would have moved
     almost nothing. #1186 changed that: themes are derived per paper now and
     the corpus holds 91 signatures, with each annual edition spanning nine to
     sixteen themes, so the frames differ.

     It sits under the map, where a time axis belongs, and it steps through
     the annual conferences only. The joint events and the Ukraine workshop
     are two, eight and ten papers, which would read as the map going blank
     rather than as a year with a different shape. They stay on the chips. #}
  <div class="atlas-controls atlas-time" id="atlas-time" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.timeAria }}">
    <span class="lbl">{{ a.timeLabel }}</span>
    {# Icon rather than the words PLAY and PAUSE (#1508). Both glyphs are in
       the markup and CSS shows one, so the script toggles an attribute
       rather than building an SVG, and the accessible name is on the button
       where a screen reader expects it. #}
    <button class="atlas-time__play" type="button" id="atlas-time-play"
            aria-label="{{ a.timePlay }}" title="{{ a.timePlay }}">
      <span class="atlas-time__icon atlas-time__icon--play" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("play") }}</span>
      <span class="atlas-time__icon atlas-time__icon--pause" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("pause") }}</span>
    </button>
    <input class="atlas-time__range" type="range" id="atlas-time-range" min="0" max="0" step="1" value="0" aria-label="{{ a.timeRangeAria }}">
    <output class="atlas-time__label" id="atlas-time-label" for="atlas-time-range">{{ a.timeEvery }}</output>
  </div>

  {# Visible list alternative (#1155, widened in #1446): the canvas is opaque
     to assistive technologies, and a plain list suits plenty of sighted
     readers too. It lists what the CURRENT view holds rather than sending
     everyone to the whole Anthology, so a filtered map has a text twin. A
     <details> rather than a visually-hidden block on purpose: hidden links
     are focusable links a sighted keyboard user cannot see, and a closed
     disclosure keeps its contents out of the tab order entirely. #}
  {# Newest first, which is the order the script uses too, so the rows do not
     reshuffle when it takes over. Nunjucks has no `contains` test for
     selectattr, so a theme page filters inside the loop instead. No cap is
     needed there: the largest theme holds 76 papers. #}
  {%- set listSorted = anthologyAtlas.papers | sort(true, false, "year") %}
  {%- set listCount = atlasTheme.count if atlasTheme else (anthologyAtlas.papers | length) %}
  <details class="atlas-list" id="atlas-list">
    <summary class="atlas-list__summary" id="atlas-list-summary">{{ a.listSummary | replace("{count}", listCount) | replace("{noun}", a.papers) }}</summary>
    {%- set listLink = '<a href="' + ('/anthology.html' | localizedHref(lang)) + '">' + a.listLedeLink + '</a>' -%}
    <p class="atlas-list__lede muted">{{ a.listLede | replace("{link}", listLink) | safe }}</p>
    {# A table rather than a list (#1496). Every row carries the same three
       fields, which is what a table is for: a screen reader can move by
       column and say which one it is in, where a list leaves "2019" as a
       fragment after a title. The header row is rewritten per lens by the
       script, since the Authors lens has its own two columns. #}
    <div class="atlas-list__scroll">
      <table class="atlas-list__table" id="atlas-list-table">
        <thead id="atlas-list-head">
          <tr><th scope="col">{{ a.colPaper }}</th><th scope="col">{{ a.colAuthors }}</th><th scope="col">{{ a.colYear }}</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="atlas-list-items">
          {%- for p in listSorted %}
          {%- if (atlasTheme and atlasTheme.name in p.themes) or (not atlasTheme and loop.index <= 200) %}
          <tr>
            <td>{% if p.url %}<a href="{{ p.url }}">{{ p.title }}</a>{% else %}{{ p.title }}{% endif %}</td>
            <td>{{ p.authors | join(", ") }}</td>
            <td>{{ p.year }}</td>
          </tr>
          {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="atlas-list__more muted" id="atlas-list-more"{% if listCount <= 200 %} hidden{% endif %}>
      {%- if listCount > 200 %}{{ a.listMore | replace("{cap}", 200) | replace("{count}", listCount) }}{% endif %}
    </p>
  </details>
  {# Theme hub panel (#1465). Clicking a hub used to do nothing at all, and on
     a touchscreen a hub had no hover card either, so the labelled circles that
     organise the whole map were the one thing on it that answered nothing.
     The panel sits under the map rather than floating over it, because at
     375px an overlay covers the cluster it is describing. The map keeps the
     hub's own highlight while the panel is open, which is what ties the two
     together. #}
  <aside class="atlas-hub" id="atlas-hub" aria-labelledby="atlas-hub-name" hidden>
    <button class="atlas-hub__close" id="atlas-hub-close" type="button" aria-label="{{ a.hubClose }}">&times;</button>
    <h2 class="atlas-hub__name" id="atlas-hub-name"></h2>
    <p class="atlas-hub__meta" id="atlas-hub-meta"></p>
    {# What the map cannot show: which themes this one actually bridges into.
       Each is a button that moves the panel across to that theme. #}
    <div class="atlas-hub__bridges" id="atlas-hub-bridges"></div>
    {# The button carries its default label in the markup rather than waiting
       for JS, or it would ship as a button with no accessible name. The two
       links are built by the script instead: a placeholder href="#" is a
       broken link on 18 pages, and there is no honest default for a link
       whose target is whichever theme you just clicked. #}
    <div class="atlas-hub__actions" id="atlas-hub-actions">
      <button class="atlas-hub__filter" id="atlas-hub-filter" type="button">{{ a.hubShowOnlyTheme }}</button>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <div class="atlas-legend" id="atlas-legend">
    <span><span class="sw sw-theme"></span>{{ a.legendColour }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-page"></span>{{ a.legendHasPage }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-nopage"></span>{{ a.legendNoPage }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-ring sw-prize"></span>{{ a.legendPrize }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-pub"></span>{{ a.legendPublished }}</span>
    <span>{{ a.hintHub }}</span>
    <span>{{ a.hintZoom }}</span>
  </div>
  <div class="atlas-legend" id="atlas-legend-authors" hidden>
    <span><span class="sw sw-collab"></span>{{ a.legendCollab }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-solo"></span>{{ a.legendSolo }}</span>
    <span><span class="sw sw-coauthor"></span>{{ a.legendCoauthored }}</span>
    <span>{{ a.legendDotSize }}</span>
    <span>{{ a.hintHub }}</span>
    <span>{{ a.hintZoom }}</span>
  </div>

  {# The corpus figures read as context for the map, so they sit under it
     rather than above it, where they were part of the 1074px of chrome a
     reader had to scroll past to reach the thing itself (#1430). #}
  <div class="atlas-stats" id="atlas-stats" role="group" aria-label="{{ a.statsAria }}"></div>

  {# Findings, not inventory (#1468). The tiles above count the corpus, which
     the Anthology can do just as well. These say what only the map's own
     structure knows, and the first is a button into the two themes it names. #}
  <div class="atlas-findings" id="atlas-findings"></div>

  {# Every theme pair at once (#1567). The hub panel answers one pair at a
     time, and 70 of the 136 possible pairs carry papers, so the structure a
     reader walks press by press has never been shown whole. Markup, not
     canvas: this is the view that survives a screenshot and a screen reader.
     Closed by default, because #1508 found the foot of this page was already
     doing too much. Canonical Atlas only: on a theme page the hub panel
     already carries that theme's own bridges, and seventeen copies of a
     289-cell table would be weight for nothing. #}
  {%- if not atlasTheme %}
  <details class="atlas-matrix">
    <summary class="atlas-matrix__summary">{{ a.matrixSummary | replace("{used}", themeMatrix.pairsUsed) | replace("{all}", themeMatrix.pairsPossible) }}</summary>
    <div class="atlas-matrix__scroll">
      <table class="atlas-matrix__table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <td></td>
            {%- for r in themeMatrix.rows %}
            {# The column head is the row number, with the theme name for
               assistive technology: seventeen full names across the top is not
               a table anybody can read, in any language. #}
            <th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">{{ r.label[L] or r.label.en }}</span><span aria-hidden="true">{{ r.number }}</span></th>
            {%- endfor %}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        {%- for r in themeMatrix.rows %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="atlas-matrix__n" aria-hidden="true">{{ r.number }}</span> <a href="/anthology-atlas/theme/{{ r.slug }}{{ themeSuffix }}.html">{{ r.label[L] or r.label.en }}</a></th>
            {%- for c in r.cells %}
            {%- if c.diagonal %}
            <td class="atlas-matrix__self" title="{{ a.matrixSelf | replace("{name}", r.label[L] or r.label.en) | replace("{n}", r.total) | replace("{noun}", a.paper if r.total == 1 else a.papers) }}">{{ r.total }}</td>
            {%- elif c.n %}
            {%- set cellLabel = a.matrixCell | replace("{a}", r.label[L] or r.label.en) | replace("{b}", c.otherLabel[L] or c.otherLabel.en) | replace("{n}", c.n) | replace("{noun}", a.paper if c.n == 1 else a.papers) %}
            {# Into the bridge view (#1468), which already has its own URL
               (#1473). The English name is the join key the map filters on,
               here as everywhere. #}
            <td class="atlas-matrix__on" style="--cell: {{ c.weight }}"><a href="{{ atlasHref }}?bridge={{ r.name | urlencode }}|{{ c.other | urlencode }}" title="{{ cellLabel }}" aria-label="{{ cellLabel }}">{{ c.n }}</a></td>
            {%- else %}
            <td></td>
            {%- endif %}
            {%- endfor %}
          </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {# The same 70 pairs ranked, which is what a phone gets: seventeen columns
       is not a table anybody can read at 375px. site.css shows exactly one of
       the two at any width, and `display: none` takes the other out of the
       accessibility tree with it, so a screen reader is never read the same
       counts twice. #}
    <ol class="atlas-matrix__ranked" aria-label="{{ a.matrixRankedAria }}">
      {%- for p in themeMatrix.pairs %}
      {%- set nameA = p.labelA[L] or p.labelA.en %}
      {%- set nameB = p.labelB[L] or p.labelB.en %}
      {%- set noun = a.paper if p.n == 1 else a.papers %}
      <li>
        {# The visible line separates the two names with a dot rather than the
           word "and": several theme names contain "and" themselves, so the
           sentence form is ambiguous on the page. Speech gets the sentence,
           through the accessible name. #}
        <a href="{{ atlasHref }}?bridge={{ p.a | urlencode }}|{{ p.b | urlencode }}"
           aria-label="{{ a.matrixCell | replace("{a}", nameA) | replace("{b}", nameB) | replace("{n}", p.n) | replace("{noun}", noun) }}">{{ nameA }} <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> {{ nameB }} <span class="atlas-matrix__count muted">{{ p.n }} {{ noun }}</span></a>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>
    <p class="atlas-matrix__note muted">{{ a.matrixNote }}</p>
  </details>
  {%- endif %}

  {# No licence block here (#1508). It went in below the map with the stats,
     the findings and the theme list, and the foot of the page was doing too
     much. The footer states the licence on every page, and the head still
     carries <link rel="license">, so nothing is lost but the crowding. The
     Anthology keeps its block, where it sits beside the citation panel it
     belongs with. #}

  {# Wayfinding between the whole map and its per-theme views (#1255).
     An unlinked page is one a crawler reaches only through the sitemap and a
     reader never reaches at all, so the canonical Atlas lists all seventeen and
     each theme page links back plus sideways to the others. Rendered from
     atlasThemePages, so a new theme in the corpus joins the list on its own. #}
  <nav class="atlas-themes" aria-label="{% if atlasTheme %}{{ a.themesNavOther }}{% else %}{{ a.themesNavHeading }}{% endif %}">
    <h2 class="atlas-themes__h">{% if atlasTheme %}{{ a.themesNavOther }}{% else %}{{ a.themesNavHeading }}{% endif %}</h2>
    <ul class="atlas-themes__list">
      {%- for t in atlasThemePages %}
      {%- if not atlasTheme or t.slug != atlasTheme.slug %}
      <li><a href="/anthology-atlas/theme/{{ t.slug }}{{ themeSuffix }}.html">{{ t.label[L] or t.label.en }} <span class="atlas-themes__n muted">{{ t.count }}</span></a></li>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    {# An identifier nobody can find is barely better than one never minted,
       and the map is where the people who would reuse this vocabulary already
       are (#1571). The nav is not the place for it, the theme list is. #}
    <p class="atlas-themes__vocab muted">
      <a href="/vocab/themes/">{{ a.themesNavVocab }}</a>
    </p>
    {%- if atlasTheme %}
    {# The query-string form stays the canonical shareable filter and is already
       indexed, so the theme page points at its equivalent rather than replacing
       it (#1255 step 6). #}
    <p class="atlas-themes__alt muted"><a href="{{ atlasHref }}?themes={{ atlasTheme.name | urlencode }}">{{ a.themesNavOpenFull }}</a></p>
    {# Visible subscribe affordance (#1256 step 4): an unadvertised feed is a
       feed nobody uses, and the <link rel="alternate"> in the head is invisible
       to anyone whose browser stopped surfacing feeds. #}
    <p class="atlas-themes__feed">
      <a href="/feeds/themes/{{ atlasTheme.slug }}.xml" type="application/atom+xml">{{ icon("bell") }} {{ a.themesNavFollow }}</a>
      <span class="muted">{{ a.themesNavFeedNote }}</span>
    </p>
    {# This theme's concept URI in the published vocabulary (#1249 / #1571).
       Shown in full rather than hidden behind link text: it is an identifier
       somebody copies into a citation or a mapping file, and a theme page is
       the likeliest place a crawler or a reader arrives at it. #}
    {%- if themeVocab.uriByName[atlasTheme.name] %}
    <p class="atlas-themes__uri">
      <span class="muted">{{ a.vocabConcept }}</span>
      <a href="{{ themeVocab.uriByName[atlasTheme.name] }}"><code>{{ themeVocab.uriByName[atlasTheme.name] }}</code></a>
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- endif %}
  </nav>
</div>

{# Design credit (#1228), on every Atlas surface (#1490). It used to render on
   the canonical map alone, on the argument that the seventeen theme pages are
   the same Atlas pre-filtered and eighteen credits is seventeen too many.
   That reads the theme pages as duplicates. They are real URLs with their own
   titles and share cards (#1255), they are the ones most likely to be shared,
   and a page naming no author is a page nobody can be asked about. One string
   from one include, not eighteen copies. EN-only, like the rest of this page. #}
{#- This include never sets `t` (archive-page.njk does, but the Atlas page does
    not), so read the catalog directly. The Atlas is EN-only, so `en` is not a
    fallback here, it is the only locale this page has. -#}
{%- set _c = i18n.en.footer.credit -%}
<p class="page-credit" data-pagefind-ignore>
  {{ _c.atlas }}
  <a href="/board.html#{{ _c.authorSlug }}">{{ _c.authorName }}</a>
</p>

<script src="{{ '/assets/js/atlas-tour.js' | bust }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/anthology-atlas.js' | bust }}" defer></script>
